window.CONTENT["m06-n03"] = {
 understand: `
 <p><b>Dentine and pulp are one organ.</b> The odontoblast's cell body sits at the edge of the pulp while its process lives inside dentine, so whatever touches dentine is felt by the pulp, and whatever the pulp does is written into dentine. Stems move from "sensitive to cold" to "which tissue responded" for that reason.</p>
 <p><b>Dentine structure.</b> About 70% mineral by weight, 20% organic (mainly type I collagen) and 10% water: softer than enamel, slightly elastic, the cushion that stops enamel shattering. Odontoblasts (dental papilla, neural crest origin) line the pulp and each leaves a process in a <b>dentinal tubule</b> running to the DEJ, S-shaped in the crown. Tubules are more numerous and wider near the pulp (about 45,000 per mm², about 2.5 µm wide) than at the DEJ (about 20,000 per mm², about 0.9 µm), so deep dentine is much more permeable: a deep cavity floor passes more bacterial products than a shallow one. <b>Intertubular dentine</b> is the collagen-based bulk; <b>peritubular dentine</b> lines each tubule and is highly mineralised and collagen-poor. The first-formed <b>mantle dentine</b> at the DEJ is slightly less mineralised; the rest is <b>circumpulpal</b>, and a band of unmineralised <b>predentine</b> always lies against the odontoblasts. Lines of von Ebner mark the daily rhythm, contour lines of Owen a disturbance, and the neonatal line birth. <b>Interglobular dentine</b> is hypomineralised where mineral globules failed to fuse (vitamin D deficiency, fluorosis); the <b>granular layer of Tomes</b> is a granular-looking zone of root dentine beneath cementum.</p>
 <p><b>Three dentines by timing.</b> <b>Primary</b> dentine forms until the root is complete. <b>Secondary</b> dentine forms slowly for life from the same odontoblasts, unevenly (more on the roof and floor of molar chambers), so the chamber shrinks and the horns recede with age: an older patient's access cavity is deeper and smaller. <b>Tertiary</b> dentine forms only under a stimulus (caries, wear, cutting). If the original odontoblasts survive a mild insult they lay <b>reactionary</b> dentine, tubular and continuous with the old. If they die, new odontoblast-like cells from the pulp lay <b>reparative</b> dentine, irregular with few or no tubules: a barrier rather than a continuation. Under slow caries or wear the tubules also fill with peritubular mineral, giving translucent, less sensitive, harder-to-bond <b>sclerotic</b> dentine. Tubules emptied by the death of their processes are <b>dead tracts</b>: air-filled, dark in transmitted light, and permeable until reparative dentine seals their pulpal ends.</p>
 <p><b>Why dentine hurts: the hydrodynamic theory</b> (Brännström). Nerve endings reach only the innermost dentine, yet cold, air, a probe or sugar on exposed dentine give sharp pain. The stimulus shifts fluid in the tubules; the shift deforms the <b>A-delta fibres</b> at the pulp-dentine border and they fire. Clinically the dentine just inside the <b>DEJ</b> is the most sensitive zone during cavity preparation, classically explained by the profuse terminal branching of tubules there (outer dentine is also more compliant, so cutting moves more fluid); permeability is a different property and is greatest near the pulp. An open tubule is a hypersensitive tubule, so treatment either plugs tubules (fluoride, oxalate, arginine, resin, or the natural <b>smear layer</b> of cutting debris) or quietens the nerve (potassium nitrate). Etching removes the smear layer and opens tubules, which is why an unsealed restoration is sensitive afterwards.</p>
 <p><b>Pulp.</b> Loose connective tissue in a rigid box, with zones from the dentine inward: odontoblast layer, <b>cell-free zone of Weil</b>, <b>cell-rich zone</b>, and the core with vessels and nerves entering through the apical foramen. Fibroblasts are the commonest cell; undifferentiated mesenchymal cells supply replacement odontoblasts. Two nerve types: fast myelinated <b>A-delta</b> (sharp, brief, better localised, hydrodynamic) and slow unmyelinated <b>C fibres</b> (dull, throbbing, poorly localised, deeper, resistant to hypoxia). The change from brief cold pain to spontaneous throbbing in pulpitis is the change from A-delta to C-fibre dominance. The pulp has no proprioceptors, so its pain is poorly localised and often referred. Because the walls cannot yield, inflammation raises pressure and compresses the thin-walled vessels: the <b>low-compliance</b> trap that can turn pulpitis into necrosis. <b>Pulp stones</b> (true, with tubules; false, concentric layers) and diffuse calcifications increase with age and irritation; usually silent, they obstruct access. With age the pulp shrinks, loses cells, gains fibres and tests less reliably.</p>
 <p><b>Why the exam cares.</b> Reactionary versus reparative, A-delta versus C, deep-cavity permeability and the hydrodynamic theory are the mechanisms behind liners, pulp capping, pulp diagnosis and desensitising toothpaste. "Sharp, short, on cold" is fluid moving in tubules.</p>`,
 connect: [
  {to:"m07-n04", why:"Secondary dentine shrinks the chamber and recedes the horns, so the canal anatomy you access in a 70-year-old is not the one in the atlas."},
  {to:"m15-n04", why:"Tubule density near the pulp and the difference between reactionary and reparative dentine decide liners, indirect pulp capping and how much dentine to leave."},
  {to:"m15-n06", why:"Dentine hypersensitivity is the hydrodynamic theory in the clinic; every desensitiser either plugs tubules or calms A-delta fibres."},
  {to:"m20-n01", why:"AAE pulp diagnoses map onto fibre physiology: brief cold pain is A-delta, lingering or spontaneous throbbing is C-fibre inflammation."},
  {to:"m20-n07", why:"Vital pulp therapy depends on the pulp's reserve of odontoblast-like cells to lay a reparative dentine bridge."}
 ],
 apply: {
  vignette: "A 38-year-old complains that cold water and breathing in winter air cause a sharp pain on the lower left that disappears within a second or two. There is no spontaneous pain. Teeth 3.3 and 3.4 show 3 mm of gingival recession with exposed, non-carious cervical dentine, and both respond briefly to cold and normally to percussion. Which of the following best explains the pain?",
  options: ["Cold conducts through dentine to the pulp core and activates C fibres","Fluid movement in open dentinal tubules deforms A-delta fibres at the pulp-dentine border","Inflammatory mediators have sensitised nerve endings within the dentinal tubules","Bacterial toxins in the tubules are directly stimulating pulpal nociceptors"],
  answer: "Fluid movement in open dentinal tubules deforms A-delta fibres at the pulp-dentine border",
  steps: [
   "Ask: a mechanism, not a diagnosis or treatment.",
   "Facts that matter: sharp, brief, stimulus-bound pain; exposed cervical dentine with no caries; no spontaneous or lingering pain; normal percussion. This is dentine hypersensitivity on a healthy pulp.",
   "Eliminate thermal conduction to C fibres: dentine is a poor conductor, C-fibre pain is dull and lingering, and the pain here is sharp and over in seconds.",
   "Eliminate inflammatory sensitisation: nothing suggests pulpitis, and nerve endings do not extend far into tubules; sensitised fibres would give lingering pain.",
   "Eliminate bacterial toxins: the dentine is non-carious; and chemical irritation would not switch on and off with a breath of air.",
   "The survivor explains everything: recession exposed tubules that are open at the surface; cold and air shift tubular fluid outward, the fluid shift mechanically deforms A-delta endings near the odontoblast layer, and they fire fast, sharp and briefly. Treatment follows: plug the tubules or calm the fibres."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt: "Explain to a patient with recession why cold hurts and why a desensitising toothpaste helps; then switch to a colleague and explain why a slowly advancing carious lesion is often less sensitive than a fast one, using reactionary dentine, sclerosis and reparative dentine.",
  model: "Exposed root dentine is full of tiny fluid-filled tubes that run to the nerve; cold or air pulls the fluid along the tubes and tugs on the nerve, which is why the pain is sharp and stops when the stimulus stops. Desensitising pastes either block the tube openings or make the nerve harder to trigger. For the colleague: a slow lesion gives surviving odontoblasts time to lay reactionary dentine behind it and to fill tubules with mineral (sclerosis), so fewer open tubules reach the pulp and fluid cannot move; a rapid lesion kills odontoblasts and leaves open dead tracts until new cells from the pulp build an irregular reparative barrier with few or no tubules."
 },
 cards: [
  ["Dentine composition by weight","About 70% mineral, 20% organic (mainly type I collagen), 10% water"],
  ["Where is dentine most permeable, and why?","Near the pulp: tubules are more numerous and wider there than at the DEJ"],
  ["Peritubular vs intertubular dentine","Peritubular lines the tubule, highly mineralised, little collagen; intertubular is the collagen-based bulk"],
  ["Primary, secondary, tertiary dentine","Primary: until root completion; secondary: slow, lifelong, shrinks the chamber; tertiary: only under a stimulus"],
  ["Reactionary vs reparative dentine","Reactionary: surviving odontoblasts, mild stimulus, tubular; reparative: new odontoblast-like cells after odontoblast death, irregular, few or no tubules"],
  ["Sclerotic dentine","Tubules occluded by mineral under slow insult; translucent, less sensitive, harder to bond"],
  ["Hydrodynamic theory in one line","Stimuli move tubular fluid; the shift deforms A-delta fibres at the pulp-dentine border; sharp brief pain"],
  ["A-delta vs C fibres","A-delta: myelinated, fast, sharp, better localised, hydrodynamic; C: unmyelinated, slow, dull, throbbing, inflammatory, hypoxia-resistant"],
  ["Pulp zones from dentine inward","Odontoblast layer, cell-free zone of Weil, cell-rich zone, pulp core"],
  ["Why does pulpitis progress to necrosis so readily?","Low-compliance environment: rigid walls, so inflammatory swelling raises pressure and strangles the vessels"],
  ["Pulp stones","True (dentine-like, tubules) or false (concentric layers); usually symptomless; complicate access"],
  ["Interglobular dentine","Hypomineralised zones where calcospherites failed to fuse; vitamin D deficiency, fluorosis"]
 ],
 questions: [
  ["The cell body of the odontoblast is located:","At the periphery of the pulp, with its process in a dentinal tubule",["Within the dentinal tubule, with its process reaching the pulp","In the cell-rich zone of the pulp","At the dentino-enamel junction, with its process reaching the pulp"],"Odontoblasts form a single layer on the pulpal surface of predentine and send one process into the tubule. This is why dentine is a living, responsive tissue despite being acellular in its bulk."],
  ["Dentine permeability is greatest:","Near the pulp, where tubules are widest and most numerous",["In outer root dentine, at the granular layer of Tomes","In the mantle dentine, the first layer formed beneath enamel","In sclerotic dentine"],"Tubules converge toward the pulp, so both their density and diameter rise; a deep cavity floor is therefore far more permeable than a shallow one. Sclerotic dentine has occluded tubules and is the least permeable; the DEJ zone is classically the most sensitive, which is a different property."],
  ["Tertiary dentine that is tubular and continuous with the original dentine, laid down by the original odontoblasts after a mild stimulus, is called:","Reactionary dentine",["Reparative dentine","Secondary dentine","Mantle dentine"],"Reactionary dentine is a response by surviving odontoblasts. Reparative dentine, the tempting alternative, is formed by newly differentiated cells after the originals die and is irregular and poorly tubular."],
  ["A 24-year-old has a rapidly progressing carious lesion on 3.6 that is excavated close to the pulp without exposure. Histologically the odontoblasts under the lesion have died. The dentine the pulp will now form beneath the cavity is most likely to be:","Reparative dentine from newly differentiated odontoblast-like cells",["Reactionary dentine formed by the original odontoblasts","Secondary dentine formed at an accelerated rate by the surviving cells","Peritubular dentine filling the tubules"],"Dead odontoblasts cannot react; progenitor cells from the pulp differentiate and lay an irregular reparative barrier with few or no tubules. Reactionary dentine requires the original cells to survive."],
  ["According to the hydrodynamic theory, pain from exposed dentine is produced by:","Fluid movement in dentinal tubules deforming nerve endings near the pulp",["Direct stimulation of nerve endings that extend to the dentine surface","Conduction of temperature changes to the pulp core","Release of neuropeptides from odontoblasts acting as sensory receptors"],"Nerve endings barely enter the tubules; fluid shift is the common pathway for cold, air, probing and osmotic stimuli. Nerves do not reach the outer dentine, so direct stimulation at the surface cannot explain the pain."],
  ["A 50-year-old reports spontaneous, dull, throbbing pain from the lower right that wakes her at night and is poorly localised. The fibres most responsible for this pain are:","Unmyelinated C fibres",["Myelinated A-delta fibres","A-beta mechanoreceptor fibres","Sympathetic efferent fibres"],"C fibres are slow, deep, poorly localised and driven by inflammatory mediators; they also survive hypoxia, so they dominate late pulpitis. A-delta fibres give sharp, brief, better-localised pain on cold."],
  ["The cell-free zone of Weil is located:","Immediately beneath the odontoblast layer",["In the centre of the pulp","Between the cell-rich zone and the pulp core","Within the predentine"],"From dentine inward the zones are odontoblast layer, cell-free zone, cell-rich zone, core. The nerve plexus of Raschkow runs through the cell-free zone."],
  ["A periapical radiograph of an unrestored, caries-free 4.6 in a 72-year-old shows a very small pulp chamber with no visible pulp horns. The most likely explanation is:","Continued deposition of secondary dentine throughout life",["Reparative dentine laid down in response to past caries","Diffuse pulp stones","Internal resorption of the pulp chamber walls"],"Secondary dentine forms slowly and asymmetrically for life, mainly on the chamber roof and floor, narrowing the chamber with age. Reparative dentine is localised to a site of injury, not a generalised reduction."],
  ["Pulp stones seen on a radiograph of an otherwise symptom-free 2.6 are best regarded as:","A usually symptomless finding that may hinder endodontic access",["A sign of pulp necrosis","A cause of spontaneous pain that requires root canal treatment","A contraindication to restoration"],"Calcifications are common, increase with age and irritation and rarely cause symptoms. They matter when access or canal location is needed, not as a diagnosis in themselves."],
  ["Which feature of the pulp environment best explains why pulpal inflammation can progress to necrosis rather than resolve like inflammation in skin?","Rigid dentine walls mean oedema raises tissue pressure and compresses vessels",["Pulpal fibroblasts cannot produce collagen to wall off the inflamed area","The pulp contains no immune cells, so bacteria spread unopposed","Pulpal nerves lack myelin"],"Low compliance is the key: swelling cannot be accommodated, pressure rises and the thin-walled vessels entering through the apex are compressed. The pulp does contain immune cells, fibroblasts make its collagen, and A-delta fibres are myelinated."],
  ["Sclerotic dentine beneath a slowly advancing cervical lesion is:","Less permeable and less sensitive, as mineral occludes the tubules",["More sensitive because its tubules have widened and lost peritubular dentine","Softer than normal dentine because it is partly demineralised","Unmineralised predentine"],"Peritubular mineral fills the tubules, blocking fluid movement; the dentine looks translucent and is harder, not softer. Bonding to it is also weaker."],
  ["Interglobular dentine is most closely associated with:","Hypomineralisation, as in vitamin D deficiency or fluorosis",["Excessive secondary dentine deposition with advancing age","Odontoblast death","Pulp stone formation in the coronal pulp"],"Mineralisation proceeds as globules (calcospherites) that normally fuse; when they fail to fuse the unmineralised matrix between them persists as interglobular dentine."],
  ["The most numerous cell type in the dental pulp is the:","Fibroblast",["Odontoblast","Macrophage","Undifferentiated mesenchymal cell"],"Fibroblasts make and maintain the collagen and ground substance of the pulp. Odontoblasts form a single layer, so they are prominent but not the majority."],
  ["Potassium nitrate in a desensitising toothpaste reduces dentine hypersensitivity by:","Reducing the excitability of pulpal nerve fibres",["Precipitating crystals that occlude tubule openings","Remineralising the exposed dentine surface","Killing bacteria within the tubules"],"Potassium ions diffuse along the tubules and depolarise the nerve endings, so a stronger stimulus is needed to fire them. Oxalates, arginine and fluoride work by the other strategy, tubule occlusion."],
  ["A patient cannot say whether the pain is from 1.6 or 4.6, yet percussion of 4.6 reproduces it exactly. The best explanation is:","Pulpal pain is poorly localised, but PDL mechanoreceptors localise the tooth",["Enamel on 4.6 is thinner, so the pulp of 4.6 is more sensitive","The maxillary and mandibular pulps share a single nerve, so pain crosses arches","The pulp of 1.6 is sclerosed and so cannot generate pain"],"Pulpal pain is referred and vague; once inflammation reaches the PDL, its mechanoreceptors localise the tooth precisely, which is why percussion is so useful."]
 ]
};

window.CONTENT["m06-n04"] = {
 understand: `
 <p><b>The tooth is suspended, not cemented in.</b> The root sits in a socket, held by a ligament whose collagen fibres are buried at one end in cementum on the root and at the other in bone. The fibres and tissue fluid turn much of the biting load into tension on the socket wall, so the apical vessels and nerves are not crushed. Periodontitis, orthodontic movement and a "widened PDL space" on a radiograph are all changes in this three-part attachment.</p>
 <p><b>Cementum.</b> A thin, avascular, uninnervated mineralised tissue on root dentine, about 45 to 50% mineral by weight, softer than dentine. It is made by cementoblasts from the dental follicle once HERS breaks up. <b>Acellular extrinsic fibre cementum</b> typically covers the cervical two-thirds, forms slowly and consists largely of the embedded ends of PDL fibres, <b>Sharpey's fibres</b>: this is the attachment cementum. <b>Cellular cementum</b> (intrinsic or mixed fibre) covers the apical third and furcations (where it can alternate with acellular layers), forms faster, traps cementoblasts as <b>cementocytes</b> in lacunae, and is the adaptive and repair tissue. Cementum is not normally remodelled; it is added to throughout life, thickest at the apex, and this apical growth compensates for occlusal wear. It resists resorption better than bone (an unmineralised precementum layer and no vessels), so orthodontic force moves the socket rather than eating the root, though heavy or prolonged force can still cause root resorption. At the <b>CEJ</b> cementum classically overlaps enamel in about 60 to 65% of teeth, meets it edge to edge in about 30%, and leaves a gap of exposed dentine in about 5 to 10%, a cause of cervical sensitivity; figures vary between studies. <b>Hypercementosis</b> is excess cementum, seen with Paget's disease, chronic periapical inflammation and unopposed teeth; it makes extraction harder. When the ligament is lost, as after a dry avulsion, the root fuses directly with bone: <b>ankylosis</b>, with a metallic percussion note, no mobility and, in a growing child, infra-occlusion.</p>
 <p><b>Periodontal ligament.</b> A dense, highly cellular tissue about 0.2 mm wide (range about 0.15 to 0.38 mm), narrower in unloaded teeth and the elderly, wider under heavy function. Principal fibre groups are named by course: <b>alveolar crest</b> (resists tilting and extrusion), <b>horizontal</b>, <b>oblique</b> (the most numerous, running from bone coronally to cementum apically, the sling that takes axial load), <b>apical</b>, and <b>interradicular</b> in multirooted teeth. Gingival fibres (dentogingival, alveologingival, circular, dentoperiosteal, <b>transseptal</b>) lie above the crest (Ten Cate groups transseptal fibres here; Newman &amp; Carranza count them as a sixth principal group); transseptal fibres run tooth to tooth across the septum, are stretched by orthodontic rotation and pull it back, which is why rotations relapse and why a fiberotomy helps. PDL fibroblasts have among the fastest collagen turnover in the body. The ligament also holds cementoblasts, osteoblasts and osteoclasts, the rests of Malassez, stem cells, and the <b>mechanoreceptors</b> that give teeth proprioception: a high restoration feels high because the ligament, not the pulp, reports position. Blood arrives from the alveolar arteries through the apex, through perforations in the socket wall, and from the gingiva.</p>
 <p><b>Alveolar bone.</b> The alveolar process exists for the teeth and resorbs when they go. The socket wall, <b>alveolar bone proper</b>, is perforated by vessels (the cribriform plate) and contains <b>bundle bone</b>, where Sharpey's fibres insert. On a radiograph it is the thin white line, the <b>lamina dura</b>, with the dark PDL space beside it; a crest within about 2 mm apical to the CEJ is healthy. Loss of lamina dura points to periapical disease or, when generalised, hyperparathyroidism; a widened PDL space to occlusal trauma, apical periodontitis or, rarely, malignancy. Cortical plates and trabecular bone support the socket; the buccal plate is thin and dehiscences are common over prominent roots.</p>
 <p><b>Remodelling and orthodontics.</b> Bone obeys its loading. Light orthodontic force compresses the ligament on one side; PDL cells and osteoblasts express <b>RANKL</b>, osteoclasts arrive within days and remove the socket wall from the ligament side (<b>frontal resorption</b>), while on the tension side osteoblasts lay new bundle bone. Heavy force shuts the vessels, the compressed ligament becomes a sterile necrotic <b>hyalinised</b> zone, and movement waits until osteoclasts from the marrow remove bone from behind (<b>undermining resorption</b>): slower, more painful, more root resorption. Drugs that stop osteoclasts, such as bisphosphonates, slow tooth movement.</p>
 <p><b>Why the exam cares.</b> Fibre groups, lamina dura, ankylosis and hypercementosis are tested directly; the pressure-tension mechanism is the whole biology of orthodontics and of occlusal trauma.</p>`,
 connect: [
  {to:"m06-n01", why:"Cementoblasts, PDL fibroblasts and the rests of Malassez all trace back to the dental follicle and the fragmenting root sheath."},
  {to:"m19-n01", why:"Periodontics begins by naming the same fibre groups, the supracrestal tissue attachment (formerly biologic width) above the bone crest."},
  {to:"m22-n05", why:"Frontal versus undermining resorption is the reason light continuous force moves teeth faster and more safely than heavy force."},
  {to:"m20-n06", why:"A dry avulsed tooth loses its ligament cells; replanting it often leads to ankylosis and replacement resorption, which the IADT guidelines try to prevent."},
  {to:"m10-n07", why:"Paget's disease produces hypercementosis and loss of lamina dura, so a bone disease shows up as a root and socket finding."}
 ],
 apply: {
  vignette: "A 9-year-old had 1.1 avulsed two years ago; it was replanted after 90 minutes dry on a shelf. Today 1.1 sits 2 mm short of the occlusal plane compared with 2.1, has no mobility and gives a high-pitched metallic sound on percussion. The radiograph shows no periodontal ligament space and root outline merging with bone. The most likely diagnosis is:",
  options: ["External inflammatory root resorption","Ankylosis with replacement resorption","Hypercementosis","Delayed physiological eruption"],
  answer: "Ankylosis with replacement resorption",
  steps: [
   "Ask: a diagnosis, and every finding in the stem should fit it.",
   "Facts that matter: a long dry time killed the PDL cells before replanting; now there is infra-occlusion in a growing child, zero mobility, a metallic percussion note and no PDL space on the film.",
   "Eliminate inflammatory resorption: that needs an infected pulp driving osteoclasts, gives a radiolucent irregular root surface with adjacent bone loss, and the tooth is usually mobile, not rigid.",
   "Eliminate hypercementosis: extra cementum bulges the root but the PDL space is preserved around it, and it does not cause a metallic note or infra-occlusion.",
   "Eliminate delayed eruption: 1.1 had already erupted before the injury; a tooth that stops moving while the neighbours and alveolus keep growing is the signature of fusion to bone.",
   "The survivor explains everything: without a living ligament, bone grew directly onto cementum (ankylosis) and is now slowly replacing the root (replacement resorption). The fused tooth cannot follow alveolar growth, so it submerges, sounds metallic and does not move."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt: "Reconstruct the pressure-tension theory of orthodontic tooth movement and use it to explain two things: why heavy force moves a tooth more slowly than light force, and why the root is not normally resorbed while the socket wall is.",
  model: "When a tooth is pushed, the ligament on the leading side is compressed and the trailing side is stretched. With light force the compressed ligament stays alive, its cells and nearby osteoblasts express RANKL, osteoclasts resorb the socket wall from the ligament surface (frontal resorption) and the tooth moves within days, while the stretched side lays down new bundle bone. Heavy force crushes the vessels, the compressed ligament dies into a hyalinised zone that contains no cells to recruit osteoclasts, and bone can only be removed from the marrow side behind it (undermining resorption), so movement stalls, hurts and risks root damage. The root escapes because cementum is covered by unmineralised precementum, has no vessels, and is far more resistant to osteoclasts than bone, so the socket gives way first."
 },
 cards: [
  ["Acellular vs cellular cementum: where and what for","Acellular: cervical two-thirds, slow, extrinsic Sharpey's fibres, attachment; cellular: apical third and furcations, fast, cementocytes, adaptation and repair"],
  ["Sharpey's fibres are","The embedded terminal portions of PDL principal fibres in cementum and bundle bone"],
  ["Relationships at the CEJ","Classically: cementum overlaps enamel about 60 to 65%, edge to edge about 30%, gap with exposed dentine about 5 to 10%"],
  ["Hypercementosis: associations and problem","Paget's disease, chronic periapical inflammation, unopposed teeth; difficult extraction"],
  ["Ankylosis: three clinical signs","No mobility, metallic percussion note, infra-occlusion in a growing patient; no PDL space on radiograph"],
  ["PDL principal fibre groups","Alveolar crest, horizontal, oblique (most numerous, resists axial load), apical, interradicular; Newman & Carranza add transseptal"],
  ["Transseptal fibres: why orthodontists care","Run tooth to tooth across the septum; stretched by rotation and cause relapse; cut in supracrestal fiberotomy"],
  ["Why does a high filling feel high?","PDL mechanoreceptors give proprioception; the pulp has none"],
  ["Lamina dura is","The radiographic white line of alveolar bone proper (bundle bone lining the socket); lost in periapical disease and hyperparathyroidism"],
  ["Bundle bone","The part of alveolar bone proper into which Sharpey's fibres insert; laid down on the tension side"],
  ["Light vs heavy orthodontic force","Light: frontal resorption from the PDL side, fast and safe; heavy: hyalinisation then undermining resorption from the marrow, slow, painful, root resorption"],
  ["Normal PDL width and what changes it","About 0.2 mm; narrower in unloaded or aged teeth, wider under heavy function or occlusal trauma"]
 ],
 questions: [
  ["The most numerous group of principal fibres in the periodontal ligament is the:","Oblique group",["Horizontal group","Alveolar crest group","Apical group"],"Oblique fibres run from bone coronally down to cementum apically and form the sling that converts axial load into tension; they make up most of the ligament."],
  ["Which periodontal ligament fibre group chiefly resists intrusive forces directed along the long axis of the tooth?","Oblique fibres",["Alveolar crest fibres","Transseptal fibres","Circular fibres"],"The oblique sling suspends the tooth so that biting force pulls on the fibres rather than crushing the apex. Alveolar crest fibres resist tilting and extrusion; transseptal fibres link adjacent teeth above the crest, and circular fibres encircle the neck of the tooth in the gingiva."],
  ["Acellular extrinsic fibre cementum is found mainly:","On the cervical two-thirds of the root",["On the apical third of the root","In furcation areas of multirooted teeth","Lining the pulp chamber"],"It forms slowly during root development and is the principal attachment cementum. Cellular cementum, which contains cementocytes, occupies the apical third and furcations."],
  ["Sharpey's fibres are:","Ends of principal fibres embedded in cementum and bone",["Elastic fibres anchoring the gingiva to the tooth","Collagen fibres within the dental pulp","Fibres attaching the reduced enamel epithelium to enamel"],"They are the anchoring ends of the ligament fibres. Bone that contains them is bundle bone; cementum that contains them is extrinsic fibre cementum."],
  ["The lamina dura seen on a radiograph corresponds histologically to:","Alveolar bone proper lining the socket",["The cementum covering the root surface","The outer cortical plate of the jaw","The periodontal ligament"],"The thin dense socket wall appears as a white line because the X-ray beam passes through it tangentially. The ligament is the dark space beside it."],
  ["Generalised loss of the lamina dura around all teeth in a 48-year-old with a history of renal stones is most suggestive of:","Hyperparathyroidism",["Chronic periodontitis","Paget's disease of bone","Fibrous dysplasia"],"Parathyroid hormone excess mobilises bone mineral and thins the socket walls throughout the mouth; renal stones point to the hypercalcaemia. Periodontitis destroys crestal bone locally rather than removing lamina dura everywhere."],
  ["A radiograph of 4.6 in a 70-year-old with Paget's disease shows bulbous roots with thickened cementum. The chief clinical significance of this finding is:","Extraction will be harder and root fracture more likely",["The tooth is non-vital and needs root canal treatment","The tooth will become mobile as the ligament widens","Root canal treatment is contraindicated in Paget's disease"],"Hypercementosis enlarges the root inside its socket and can lock it in; it says nothing about pulp vitality and does not itself cause mobility."],
  ["A tooth that is immobile and produces a high-pitched metallic sound on percussion most likely has:","Ankylosis, with cementum fused directly to alveolar bone",["Acute apical periodontitis with a widened ligament space","Hypercementosis thickening the apical root","A thickened periodontal ligament"],"Without a ligament the percussion energy passes straight into bone, giving the metallic note. Apical periodontitis gives a dull note with tenderness and often mobility."],
  ["A heavy orthodontic force is applied to 2.3. During the first two weeks the tooth hardly moves. The best explanation is:","Ligament hyalinisation, forcing slow undermining resorption from the marrow side",["The oblique fibres have been torn and must reattach before movement","Cementum has been resorbed faster than the bone of the socket wall","Osteoblasts on the pressure side have been activated and are laying down new bone"],"Heavy force crushes the ligament vessels; the cell-free hyalinised zone cannot recruit osteoclasts, so resorption must come from behind and movement stalls. With light force, frontal resorption from the ligament side moves the tooth within days."],
  ["With light continuous orthodontic force, bone on the pressure side is resorbed mainly because:","Ligament cells and osteoblasts express RANKL, recruiting osteoclasts",["Osteocytes die and the socket wall fractures under the load","Cementoblasts convert to osteoclasts and resorb the socket wall","The ligament fibres pull fragments of bone away from the socket"],"RANKL binding to RANK on osteoclast precursors drives their differentiation; osteoprotegerin opposes it. Mechanical pulling by fibres does not remove bone."],
  ["During normal orthodontic tooth movement the socket wall is resorbed but the root usually is not, because:","Cementum covered by unmineralised precementum resists osteoclasts better than bone",["Dentine is harder than bone, so osteoclasts cannot attach to the root surface","The root is protected by a smear layer left on its surface","The pulp secretes inhibitors of osteoclasts through the dentinal tubules"],"Osteoclasts need a mineralised surface and a signalling environment; precementum and the ligament's cementoblasts shield the root. Heavy or prolonged force can overcome this and cause root resorption."],
  ["The average width of the healthy periodontal ligament space is approximately:","0.2 mm",["0.02 mm","1 mm","2 mm"],"The ligament is about 0.15 to 0.38 mm wide, averaging around 0.2 mm, narrower in unloaded teeth and the elderly and wider under heavy function. A width of 0.02 mm or 1 mm lies well outside that range."],
  ["After orthodontic correction of a rotated 1.2, the tooth relapses despite good alignment of the rest of the arch. The structures most responsible are:","Transseptal and other gingival fibres that remain stretched",["Hyalinised zones that persist in the periodontal ligament space","Sharpey's fibres anchored in the bundle bone","Cementocytes in cellular cementum"],"Gingival and transseptal fibres remodel very slowly and keep pulling the tooth back, which is why a supracrestal fiberotomy or prolonged retention is used after rotations. Ligament fibres reorganise within months, and hyalinised zones are cleared during tooth movement rather than pulling the tooth back."],
  ["Bundle bone is best defined as:","The part of alveolar bone proper into which Sharpey's fibres insert",["The cortical plate on the buccal aspect of the alveolar process","Trabecular bone between the cortical plates","The thickened bone of hypercementosis"],"Bundle bone lines the socket and anchors the ligament; it is the bone laid down on the tension side during tooth movement."],
  ["A new crown on 3.6 feels 'high' to the patient, although the pulp of 3.6 was removed during root canal treatment. The sensation arises from:","Mechanoreceptors in the periodontal ligament",["Residual pulpal nerve fibres in the apical canal","Nerve endings in cementum","Gingival sulcus nociceptors"],"Proprioception belongs to the ligament, which is why a pulpless tooth still senses contact. Cementum has no innervation and the pulp is gone."]
 ]
};
